Source: world-keyboard-windows
Section: x11
Priority: optional
Maintainer: @MAINTAINER@
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13)
Standards-Version: 4.6.2
Rules-Requires-Root: no

Package: world-keyboard-windows
Architecture: all
Depends: xkb-data, ${misc:Depends}
Description: extra keyboard layouts that type like Windows (prototype)
 PROTOTYPE, not released. Adds new keyboard layouts that type, key for key,
 what the same layout types on Windows, next to the Linux layouts, which
 stay as they are. Each new layout is the Linux layout with only the keys
 changed where Windows differs (Caps Lock behaviour, a few AltGr
 characters), generated from Unicode CLDR 43 Windows keyboard data and
 checked by machine. Not covered: Ctrl+Alt as AltGr.
 .
 Layouts in this prototype (60), each named "(as on Windows)": German,
 Swiss German and French, Polish (Programmers), French, English (UK),
 Irish, Spanish, Portuguese, Portuguese (Brazil, ABNT2 and ABNT), Italian
 (and 142), Hungarian (and 101-key), Estonian, Lithuanian (and IBM),
 Icelandic, Faroese, Albanian, Ukrainian (and Enhanced), French (Canada,
 legacy), Norwegian, Danish, Finnish, Swedish (Finnish, Swedish and
 Norwegian with Sami), Dutch, Luxembourgish, Maltese (47-key), Guarani,
 Hausa, Turkmen, Azerbaijani (Latin, Cyrillic), Bashkir, Tatar (and
 legacy), Tajik, Arabic (101, 102, 102 AZERTY), Kurdish (Central, Arabic
 script), Persian, Urdu, Hindi (INSCRIPT, Traditional), Gujarati,
 Kannada, Malayalam, Odia, Punjabi (Gurmukhi), Telugu, Khmer (NIDA), Lao,
 Vietnamese, Berber (Tifinagh, extended).
 .
 Optional, per user: "world-keyboard-windows accent-space on" makes an
 accent followed by Space give the accent itself, as on Windows (Linux
 gives an apostrophe or a quote). It writes a small ~/.XCompose and never
 changes one of the user's own.
 .
 It does not touch the belgian-azerty-windows package, the rules or any
 setting. To make the layouts selectable in Cinnamon (Linux Mint), which
 reads only xkb-data's layout list, it diverts that list (rules/evdev.xml)
 and rebuilds it as xkb-data's file plus its own entries; removal puts
 xkb-data's file back. Installing it only makes the layouts available.
